The bias distributions across topologies are visualized in Figure~\ref{fig:bias_distribution}, where the exposure panel's dominant red region (sign reversal) contrasts with the symmetric attenuation patterns of other topologies.

\subsection{ASV Framework Validation}
\label{sec:asv_results}

\paragraph{Envelope monotonicity.}
The ASV envelope (bias magnitude as a function of $\delta$) is monotonically non-decreasing for all seven topologies, with zero violations across all tested scenarios. This validates the theoretical monotonicity property and confirms that $\delta$ serves as a meaningful ``dial'' controlling annotation error severity.

\paragraph{Magnitude ASV.}
Table~\ref{tab:asv_grid} reports magnitude ASV ranges at $\tau{=}0.5$ with a 10\% bias threshold. The ranking from most fragile to most robust is: front-door (0.064--0.387) $<$ mediation (0.076--0.454) $<$ confounding (0.081--0.487) $<$ IV (0.101--0.607) $<$ collider (0.338--2.028) $<$ M-bias ($\infty$). The collider's high ASV reflects its protective nature: misclassification attenuates the spurious association rather than amplifying it. M-bias achieves infinite ASV because annotation error on the collider variable $A$ only reduces the already-small bias from the M-shaped confounding path.

\paragraph{Significance and sign-flip ASV.}
At $\tau{=}0.05$, significance ASV achieves 100\% power for confounding, front-door, and M-bias, but 0\% for mediation, collider, and IV (correct behavior for attenuating topologies; Type~I error is 0\%). For sign-flip ASV, 83.4\% of exposure reference matrices yield $\tau_{\text{sign\_flip}} < 1$ (catastrophic sensitivity); IV sign-flip ASV ranges 1.1--6.7 (moderate-to-high). The complete ranking (Figure~\ref{fig:asv_ranking}, Table~\ref{tab:asv_grid}, Appendix~\ref{app:supplementary}) confirms IV as maximally fragile and M-bias as immune (ASV $= \infty$).

\paragraph{$N$-sensitivity.}
The significance ASV exhibits a sharp phase transition at a critical sample size $N^* \approx 6{,}000$--$10{,}000$ (analytically computable per topology), below which all rejections are blocked.

\subsection{Binarization Collapse}
\label{sec:binarization}

Collapsing $K{=}3$ annotations to binary introduces additional bias varying 154$\times$ across topologies: from $\bar{\Delta}{=}0.006$ (M-bias) to $\bar{\Delta}{=}0.926$ (IV). Exposure and IV also introduce sign flips during binarization (14\% and 17.5\%), a phenomenon absent in the other topologies. Full results are in Appendix~\ref{app:supplementary} (Figure~\ref{fig:binarization}).

\subsection{Empirical Validation}
\label{sec:empirical}

We validate using real LLM confusion matrices from three tasks: HumAID~\cite{alam2021humaid} ($K{=}10$), VAST ($K{=}3$), and CivilComments ($K{=}2$). Four LLMs (Llama-4-Maverick, Qwen3-235B, DeepSeek-Chat, Qwen2.5-7B-Instruct) produce 20 configurations across the three datasets under zero-shot and few-shot prompting, yielding 140 bias scenarios ($20 \times 7$ topologies).

\paragraph{HumAID ($K{=}10$): theory--practice alignment.}
The empirical bias ranking matches predictions: IV (12.4--42.1\%), exposure (9.0--17.4\%), mediation/front-door (10.7--14.8\%), while collider, confounding, and M-bias remain below 10\%---consistent with the ASV vulnerability spectrum.

\paragraph{Cross-$K$ comparison.}
Bias varies substantially across settings: VAST ($K{=}3$) produces 68.2\% mean bias with IV reaching 328\%, versus 12.2\% for HumAID ($K{=}10$) and 32.4\% for CivilComments ($K{=}2$). Even CivilComments configurations with $F_1{=}0.82$ (Qwen3-235B) produce 62--92\% IV bias, confirming that classification accuracy does not predict downstream causal risk (Figure~\ref{fig:f1_vs_bias}).\footnote{VAST's near-degenerate confusion matrices (Neutral-class recall 3.6--14.3\%) drive its extreme IV bias via the Wald estimator's small-denominator amplification.}

Checking all $K{-}1$ exposure coefficients, 11 of 20 configurations (55\%) exhibit sign reversal in at least one coefficient---all in the exposure topology, confirming that exposure vulnerability persists at the coefficient level even for diagonally-dominant matrices. For HumAID ($K{=}10$), 7 of 8 configurations reverse a secondary coefficient ($|\beta| \leq 0.2$); for VAST ($K{=}3$), 4 of 5 reverse $\beta_2{=}{-}0.50$. The primary coefficient ($\beta_1$, largest magnitude) never reverses across any empirical configuration; sign flips occur exclusively in secondary coefficients corresponding to rare categories with small $|\beta|$. Sign reversal requires the DGP to contain opposite-sign treatment effects across categories (Equation~\ref{eq:exposure}); when all $\beta_k$ share the same sign, misclassification produces only attenuation. Zero sign reversals occur across 120 non-exposure scenarios ($20 {\times} 6$ topologies), validating the structural guarantee $K$-invariantly. CivilComments ($K{=}2$) confirms the binary algebraic protection. The 7B-to-frontier bias ratio is 2.38, indicating topology rather than model scale determines downstream bias.

\paragraph{ASV diagnostic results.}
For HumAID ($K{=}10$, 56 scenarios), 55.4\% are flagged as magnitude-ASV-unsafe---IV, mediation, front-door, and exposure all trigger, while confounding, collider, and M-bias remain below 10\% bias (Figure~\ref{fig:asv_heatmap}). For CivilComments ($K{=}2$), 100\% of scenarios are ASV-unsafe despite $F_1$ ranging from $0.69$ (7B) to $0.82$ (frontier).

Overlaying all 20 empirical matrices onto the theoretical Dirichlet violation-rate curves (Figure~\ref{fig:dirichlet_overlay}, Appendix~\ref{app:supplementary}) confirms placement: VAST in the danger zone, HumAID mid-range, CivilComments approaching safe for exposure but still in IV amplification.

\begin{figure}[t]
\centering
\includegraphics[width=\columnwidth]{fig5_f1_vs_bias.pdf}
\caption{Topology determines downstream causal bias, not $F_1$. HumAID $K{=}10$ (56 points): within $F_1$ range 0.614--0.690, bias varies $10{\times}$ (IV 42.1\% vs.\ collider $<$5.6\%).}
\label{fig:f1_vs_bias}
\end{figure}


\subsection{Correction Guidance}
\label{sec:correction}

We evaluate five correction methods (naive plug-in, MLA, DSL~\cite{egami_hinck_stewart_wei2023}, MC-SIMEX, PPI++~\cite{angelopoulos2023}) across all seven topologies under six error structures (210 configurations; full results in Table~\ref{tab:correction}, Appendix~\ref{app:supplementary}).

\paragraph{FWL invariance.}
For four topologies where $A$ enters as a covariate (confounding, mediation, collider, M-bias), the Frisch--Waugh--Lovell theorem implies that applying $C^{-1}$ to the dummy variables cannot change the $T$ coefficient. Naive plug-in and MLA achieve exactly 0.0\% bias reduction across these four topologies.

\paragraph{Method ranking.}
Measuring RMSE reduction ($1 - \text{RMSE}_{\text{corr}} / \text{RMSE}_{\text{uncorr}}$), PPI++ achieves the highest grand-mean reduction (47.0\%) but requires gold labels and is catastrophically harmful for M-bias ($-$88.0\%). MC-SIMEX provides gold-free correction (13.3\% grand mean, best for IV at 59.4\%) but harms front-door ($-$62.4\%). DSL corrects confounding (48.0\%) and mediation (52.9\%) but amplifies M-bias ($-$267\%) and IV ($-$206\%). No single method dominates: correction effectiveness is itself topology-dependent. Cross-PPI~\cite{zrnic_candes2024} eliminates the gold-label requirement by using two independent LLM annotators whose predictions serve as mutual references, yielding valid confidence intervals under an annotator-independence assumption. Because it operates on estimator outputs rather than on $C$ directly, Cross-PPI is not subject to FWL invariance and can potentially correct all seven topologies. Methods that bypass FWL through a different route---such as EM imputation of latent true labels and Bayesian measurement error models---offer further alternatives for the four FWL-invariant topologies.

Empirical validation on HumAID ($K{=}10$, 30 MC replicates) confirms topology-dependent method superiority: MC-SIMEX outperforms PPI++ in 4 of 7 topologies and reverses from harmful ($K{=}3$) to beneficial ($K{=}10$). A retrospective case study applying the full framework to \citet{lee_wood_doughty2024} is in Appendix~\ref{app:case_study}.

